<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/glossary#grenzer">Glossary</a> · Term</p><h1 class="enc-title">Grenzer</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Grenzers, Military Frontiersmen</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Grenzer were the soldier-settlers of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/habsburg-dynasty">Habsburg</a> Military Frontier, the border zone against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottoman Empire</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/croatia">Croatia</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavonia">Slavonia</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hungary">Hungary</a> and later <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/transylvania">Transylvania</a>. Many were <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eastern-orthodox-church">Orthodox</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serbs">Serbs</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vlachs">Vlachs</a> who fled Ottoman territory and received land, freedom from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serfdom">serfdom</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/religious-tolerance">religious tolerance</a> in return for lifelong military service. Living in communal households, they also served as light infantry in the wars of the Habsburg army until the frontier was abolished in 1881.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/grenzer.jpg" alt="Grenzer" width="320" height="456" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Serbian_frontiersman_in_Syrmia,_1742.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
